{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>Scoping mutation testing so it stays a signal instead of becoming noise</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Point mutation testing at specific folders or file globs, the payment logic, auth, anything handling money or data integrity, rather than the whole repository. Presentational components and low-risk utility code get real value from unit and integration tests already; running mutation analysis on them mostly generates noise, mutants that are technically survived but practically meaningless, without adding confidence anyone will actually use.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Most mutation tools support incremental runs, only re-mutating files that changed since the last run, which keeps a scoped setup fast enough to run on every pull request touching those directories. A lighter alternative for teams not ready for that: run it as a nightly or periodic job against the critical modules only, rather than blocking every PR, trading immediacy for still catching regressions before they compound.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Treat the mutation score target itself as scoped, too, not a single number for the whole codebase. A high bar, 85&ndash;90% or higher, makes sense for the money-handling and auth modules where a survived mutant is genuinely expensive; that same bar applied uniformly elsewhere just produces busywork chasing equivalent mutants that can't be killed by any test, however well-written. The discipline that keeps this sustainable is the same one that keeps an E2E suite trustworthy: match the tool's cost to where a gap in test quality actually matters, not to how much code exists.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

